I think so many people are willing to  turn their back on the president does he  have a loyalty issue well I do think I  mean we're talking about a limited  number of individuals right we're  talking about a handful of folks  probably that have left and written the  kinds of books that you're talking about  cliff Sims was one that you know I've  worked you know I wouldn't say alongside  him but I worked near him from time to  time you know he was a mid-level staffer  who kind of has glorified his role and  painted a picture that I don't think  reflects the white house that I saw and  I don't think he was in a lot of the  meetings and a lot of the things that he  kind of depicts in the book I mean they  just kind of strains credulity if you  read it and there are other examples  that that kind of exists you know I  can't explain what goes through the head  of a staff member who is gonna go out  and say all these negative things about  the place you work at if that's the case  I think it's a privilege to work in the  White House  you should leave and nothing compares to  the pressures to the sustained kind of  scrutiny and the challenges that people  face in a White House you know there  you're gonna break some eggs there's  gonna be you know a shouting match here  and there did you witness some of those  shouting matches as you say yeah there  been there been quite a few I mean you  know people get passionate about policy  about issues about personnel decisions  and you can get hot sometimes it is a  very dangerous time for journalists so  you concerned the divisive rhetoric  could ultimately risk journalists lives  well I mean that's I think there can  always be a concern you know I I don't  pretend to fully grasp all the other  risks associated with being a journalist  in parts of the world that don't afford  the kinds of protections that we have in  the United States and I think in in  general it's always good to see the  temperature turn down a little bit I  mean my boss my former boss Sarah  Sanders like she can't go you know out  in Washington DC without being accosted  by somebody you know I mean like that's  a terrible situation  she has three young children I you know  my heart goes out to  and I don't think there's a lot of like  sympathy or people concerned about her  welfare I think she's the first White  House press secretary that's been  required to have Secret Service  protection in the history of the country  so that goes to tell you that I think  that in general the the heat in the  situation with some of the rhetoric is a  little too high and I think that's  unfortunate  we also live in a free country and so I  think there are actions that obviously  cross the line and you know those have  to be dealt with when they happen you  worked at the RNC  you have a lot of experience in  opposition research you were reported to  have described the president at one  point as a deplorable have you changed  your mind yeah I have and I I think I  think some of that stuff was taken a  little bit out of context in the height  of a in a height of a you know bad news  cycle you know a lot of us were kind of  taken aback by the president's campaign  by a lot of things that he was doing and  I you know he won me over he won a lot  of Republicans over over the course of  that primary and you know I'm very proud  of the the time that I've served in this  White House some of the big things that  were able to do on tax reform on you  know the Supreme Court on regulatory  issues I mean his whole bevy of things  that we've worked on that I'm very proud  of so anything I may have said you know  and the kind of the heat of the moment  years ago you know I probably I probably  want to take a have a few take backs you  know you were working for a president  who can often announce policy by tweets  how is that managing messaging when you  don't necessarily know what's coming  well I think you know the president kind  of used social media in a way that is  unprecedented may never be used that way  again right I mean he was making news on  daily basis more often than not the  staff and the team was aware to a large  degree about where things were going but  I mean yeah at times it caught us a  little bit off guard and we kind of had  to kind of roll with it and that's just  sort of the nature of this White House  this president everybody just kind of  you know rolls with the fact that  he can make decisions that kind of any  point that that he deems fit any  particular moments that stick in your  mind that took me by surprise they were  quite a few is there quite a concerted  effort to try and find these strategic  phrases whether it's radical Democrats  or its enemy of the people well I would  say in general the president is you know  kind of a branding genius on his own we  saw that throughout the campaign framing  some of his political opponents in ways  that were very effective and I think  quite cutting at times so I think you  know I think he'll take input but he  seems to be able to figure those things  out pretty well himself you oversaw the  communications behind Brett Cavanaugh's  nomination of course later appointed  Supreme Court justice that was a big  success for the president were there  times during the Brett Kavanaugh  nomination process when you were worried  that actually the tide might be turning  against him well I mean I've been enough  I've been in enough situations to  understand that sometimes when it looks  bleak it's usually not as bad as it  looks as it does when it looks good it's  usually not as good as it looks right  but yeah I mean I think that any casual  observer had to look at the situation at  times and be concerned you know one of  the things that I found to be very  reassuring and I've been in probably  about now about four or five really  contentious confirmation battles or  personnel matters of this sort is the  people that had known Brett Kavanaugh  for years had stood by him from  different parts of his life whether you  know his time and executive service or  the clerk's that worked with him or  people that went to high school with him  none of them ever flinched when to  asking to be character witnesses when I  mean we're talking about hundreds of  people here I've never seen that before  we're now at the point of the State of  the Union why do you think he still  wants to take part in what is really  conventional stagecraft for a president  well I think that it is conventional but  they're also thinking  is a unique thing to the presidency that  kind of placed as advantage right he'll  have a hour maybe hour and a half long  you know prime time fixated audience  that is not going to be taking in his  message through the filter of the media  or cable news or kind of the chatter in  class he gets to talk directly to the to  the country and he can make his case in  a way that that only he is is kind of  empowered to make it looking back is  there anything that you regret that you  would have done differently or you  thought could have been managed better I  mean probably dozens you know I'll let  that I'll let that go to the people that  read my emails you know ten years from  what they're when they're released by  the presidential records folks I think  look the the pace and speed of press  coverage today pace and speed with which  we have to react to these issues forces  us to make lots of snap second decisions  and I think there's there's plenty that  you could have done a better job we do  the best job we can with the information  we have and then kind of have to move on
 

